A1. A nickname we all love (two words). ~ SAINTNICK
A8. What dad decorated at table. ~ SEAT
A10. The old Yale spirit. ~ ALE
A11. A blanket Santa needs on his trip. ~ SNOW
A14. Children get drumsticks; these get wings. ~ ANGELS
A16. Something fishy here. ~ CAVIAR
A18. A tree that's very fitting these days. ~ SHOE
A19. What the gobbler did on the farm. ~ ET
A20. Kristmas Turkeys (init.). ~ KT
A21. This gets lit during the holidays. ~ TREE
A23. Hubby was annoyed to see his mother in this. ~ LAW
A24. Full of holiday cheer. ~ GAY
A25. One of the main places Santa is seen going down (abbr., plur.). ~ STS
A27. The first thing dad did at the table. ~ SAT
A28. This started arguing Xmas eve. ~ AR
A29. This cleans up on Xmas morning. ~ MOP
A30. The children saw this in a playhouse. ~ ACT
A32. A lean too. ~ TO
A33. Mrs. Santa Claus. ~ MA
A34. The first meal of the day. ~ OAT
A35. Grunt made by rabid rooter. ~ RAH
A36. Holy Night (init.). ~ HN
A37. Button your lip! ~ TUT
A38. Boloney. ~ ROT
A40. What an Indian girl says when she means "yes" (plur.). ~ UGHS
A42. A famous Coward. ~ NOEL
A45. What Dad found in his stocking Xmas morning (plur.). ~ TOES
A46. Dumb look. ~ GAPE
A47. This takes lots of cultivation (plur.). ~ ACRES
A49. Five of the first letters for Santa. ~ SSSSS
A50. To have in Scotland. ~ HAE
A51. A well-balanced food item. ~ PEA
A54. Prof. Bird. ~ OWL
A56. Tiny totterer. ~ TOT
A57. Mother finally had to put her foot down on this at dinner. ~ BELL
A58. What you do a turkey trot to Xmas day. ~ MEAL
A60. Rather—. ~ THAN
A61. From one Christmas to the next—according to the kiddies. ~ EON
A63. After dinner pickups. ~ NAPS
A64. Santa's runners. ~ STOCKINGS

D1. This shows wise taste in dressing. ~ SAGE
D2. What little Willie did before dinner. ~ ATE
D3. This can precede a cold or a fall. ~ NASTY
D4. Compliments of the season (abbr.). ~ TL
D5. Turkey gobblers. ~ NECKS
D6. Costly scented violets (init.). ~ CSV
D7. Grandmother is this kind of wit. ~ KNIT
D8. Dumb Dora suggested this for a swell dessert. ~ SAHARA
D9. This is enough for any poet. ~ ENOW
D12. Brother asked for a pair of these to pull on. ~ OARS
D13. This went and got hung Christmas day. ~ WREATH
D15. Made a jump in his best suit. ~ LEAPT
D17. There's some scents to this present. ~ ATTAR
D18. A grand thing mother made right at the table. ~ SLAM
D22. The jacket to wear to Xmas dinner. ~ ETON
D24. A best smeller. ~ GOAT
D26. The end of the cut. ~ SCAR
D29. A bully dessert. ~ MOUSSE
D31. Bonds without stocks. ~ THONGS
D37. Not here. ~ THERE
D39. This is eaten at breakfast and drunk at night. ~ TOAST
D40. The state polygamists find themselves in. ~ UTAH
D41. Where Jr. gets a roll between meals (two words). ~ GOCART
D43. This has its downs in England (plur.). ~ EPSOMS
D44. This comes before we forget. ~ LEST
D48. What Pop feels like after Christmas. ~ SPENT
D49. Brother came home from college with the last word in this. ~ SLANG
D52. A good highway to travel. ~ EL
D53. There's nothing smarter than this. ~ ALEC
D54. A present that's everywhere (comb. form). ~ OMNI
D55. Us. & Co. ~ WE
D57. This is always a relief at any time. ~ BAS
D59. Cheer up—this is almost last. ~ LAS
D62. "All's well with the world." ~ OK


